<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haʻamonga ʻa Maui</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ha'amonga 'a Maui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haʻamonga ʻa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> is a stone trilithon on the north-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a>, Tonga. Two upright slabs of coral limestone, about five metres high, support a lintel set into notches at their tops. Tongan tradition credits its construction to the Tuʻi Tonga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuitatui">Tuʻitātui</a> around 1200, possibly as a gateway to his royal compound. In 1967 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">Tāufaʻāhau</a> Tupou IV proposed that marks on the lintel align with sunrise at the solstices.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui.jpg" alt="Haʻamonga ʻa Maui" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tauʻolunga · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ha%CA%BBamonga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
